f Danville's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Danville has set <strong>86 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 92 that chance alone would give, and <strong>78 record highs</strong>, close to the 90 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (60). Danville's all-time high is 107\u00b0F, on August 6, 1918.","t_chg":"Danville's days and nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1977","p_chg":"Across 98 complete years: Danville's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.10\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.19 to \u22120.02), about \u22121.1\u00b0F since 1917. Danville's average high has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.09\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.17 to \u22120.00), about \u22120.9\u00b0F since 1917. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1977, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.62\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.44 to +0.80) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.39\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.13 to +0.64). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; January fell. Highs rose in none; 4 months fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Danville's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.21 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 44.7 inches a year. The wettest year was 2018 (67.61 in); the driest, 1941 (24.88 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 16 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}